'''Bolodun''' is the largest island of the [[Bolodun and Kuvari Islands]], and one of the two provinces of the country. It covers approximately 306 square kilometres, about 58 percent of the country's land area, and had an estimated population of 73,643 in 2025. The island is home to the capital city, [[Ominira]], the only city on the island, which occupies about 48 percent of Bolodun's area on its western side. The island also contains the country's main airport, on the eastern half.

== Geography ==

Bolodun is a long, narrow island running roughly north-northeast to south-southwest, about 77 kilometres long and only 4–6 kilometres wide on average. Its high ratio of coastline to land area reflects this elongated crescent shape. The island lies in the northern part of the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]], separated from the smaller islands of the [[Kuvari]] chain to the south.

The island's coastline is smooth and uninterrupted, with no natural bays or deep harbours. Early landing was difficult: canoes and later sailing vessels had to be beached directly on the shore, and settlement concentrated where the sea was calmest. This shaped Bolodun's early history as a place that was reachable by sea but not easy to dock at, and contributed to the Kuvari islands' character as trade contacts rather than major ports.

=== Geology ===

Bolodun is the exposed remnant of an ancient volcanic edifice that subsided over millions of years. Coral reefs grew over the drowned volcanic foundation, and subsequent uplift raised the reef into a low-lying limestone cap over the volcanic core. The result is a mostly low and flat island with gentle limestone terraces, in contrast to the higher volcanic islands further west such as [[Guajicutea]]. The island's soils, derived from weathered volcanic rock beneath the limestone, are fertile and well suited to plantation agriculture, which made Bolodun the centre of the country's historic sugar economy.

=== Hurricanes ===

Bolodun lies in a hurricane-prone part of the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]], and the island was heavily affected by hurricanes for most of its history. The worst disaster came in 1969, when [[Hurricane Sahan]] struck the island and destroyed virtually the entire built environment, leveling Ominira and the island's infrastructure. In the aftermath, the [[Adonian Community]] led a major reconstruction of the island. During the 1970s, [[Burawa]] invested heavily in hurricane defence infrastructure as part of this rebuilding.

Since that time many structures on the island, including most of the main city, have been built raised above the ground. Urban construction in [[Ominira]] therefore consists largely of buildings elevated on stilts or columns, with the city's public life conducted essentially on the second floor. The ground floor beneath the raised city is left open and is used in non-rainy seasons for moving goods and freight through the city, sheltered from the sun. During heavy rains and storm surge, the raised city keeps its streets and utilities above flood level.

== History ==

Bolodun was reached by the expedition of [[Guled Badweyn]] in 1520 and was incorporated into the [[Badweyn Islands]] colony. Its fertile soils and flat terrain made it the natural centre of the sugar plantation economy, and in 1695 the island was separated administratively from the rest of the colony and given its own governor.

Following emancipation in 1789, non-contracted emancipated slaves were relocated to Bolodun, which was granted autonomous control under [[Iacob Kheme]]. The influx of freed Sudanese Kaftians led to the [[Kuvari War]] (1796–1801), after which Bolodun was administered by the Sudanese Kaftian majority while the Kuvari were expelled to [[Tumala]] and the outer islands. The island later formed part of the [[Capuyaquiran League]] and the [[United Capuyaquiran Republic]], and since independence in 1926 has been the larger of the country's two provinces.

In 1969, [[Hurricane Sahan]] destroyed virtually the entire island. The subsequent [[Adonian Community]]-led reconstruction rebuilt Ominira and the island's infrastructure to a raised, hurricane-resistant design supplied with heavy investment from [[Burawa]] in the 1970s.

== Demographics ==

Bolodun had an estimated population of 73,643 in 2025, about 64 percent of the national total. The population is predominantly [[Sudanese Kaftians of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|Sudanese Kaftian]], reflecting the island's history as the centre of the freed-slave administration. Most residents live in or around [[Ominira]], which occupies much of the western half of the island.

== Transport ==

The national capital [[Ominira]] is the island's principal settlement and port. Because Bolodun has no natural harbour, the port at Ominira is artificial, built to serve the sugar export trade. The country's main international airport, [[Ominira International Airport]], is located on the eastern half of the island, away from the city, connected to Ominira by road across the island.

Much of the movement of goods on the island reflects the raised-city design: in dry seasons freight moves along the open ground levels beneath the elevated buildings, while the second-floor level carries pedestrians and the main circulation of the city. The port, airport, and eastern plantations are linked by roads that follow the low central corridor of the island.

